Explain the six models in ICC

## Three different choice

1. Single vs Average

- Single: reliability of one rater's rating

- Average: reliability of the mean of k raters' rating

Average ICC is usually higher because averaging reduces measurement error

2. Model 1/2/3

- ICC1: one-way random-effects model. Raters are random; 
        usually each subjet may be rated by a different set of raters; 
        or rater identity is not modeled

- ICC2: two-way random-effects models. Same raters rate all subjects;
        Raters are considered a random sample from a larger population
        Rating can be generalized to other similar raters

- ICC3: two-way mixed-effect model. Same raters rate all subjects; 
        these speciic raters are fixed/only ones of interest
        Can't generalized to new raters

3. Absolute agreement vs Consistency

- Absolute agreement: raters must give the same absolute scores. 
                      Systematic differences between raters count as disagreement.

- Consistency: raters must rank/order subjects similarly; 
               systematic rater mean differences are allowed.

## A summary table

| Output row | ICC notation | Model | Unit | Agreement type | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|---|---|---|
| Single_raters_absolute | ICC1 = ICC(1,1) | One-way random | Single rater | Absolute | Reliability of one rating when raters are random / one-way design. |
| Single_random_raters | ICC2 = ICC(2,1) | Two-way random | Single rater | Absolute | Reliability of one rating from a random sample of raters; penalizes rater mean differences. |
| Single_fixed_raters | ICC3 = ICC(3,1) | Two-way mixed | Single rater | Consistency | Reliability of one rating from these fixed raters; ignores systematic rater mean differences. |
| Average_raters_absolute | ICC1k = ICC(1,k) | One-way random | Average of k raters | Absolute | Reliability of the mean of k ratings under one-way random model. |
| Average_random_raters | ICC2k = ICC(2,k) | Two-way random | Average of k raters | Absolute | Reliability of the mean of k ratings, generalizing to other similar raters. |
| Average_fixed_raters | ICC3k = ICC(3,k) | Two-way mixed | Average of k raters | Consistency | Reliability of the mean of k ratings for these fixed raters only. |

